    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d398">

                <pb facs="0778" n="682" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg682"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.6363/1918: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sheffield</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-08-06T13:00:00-07:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 6,
                                1926—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received August 7—12:38
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>329. Embassy’s despatch No. 2474, July l.<anchor n="68" xml:id="d398-f1"/> I have
                    been informed by representatives of two of the leading British oil companies
                    that they are presenting a modified memorandum to the Mexican Department of
                    Industry regarding the drilling permit order of June 8. They have been given to
                    understand that the memorandum will be accepted and if so the order of June 8
                    will be virtually nullified. They believe that this changed attitude is due to
                    the firm position assumed in our notes of June 22 and July 21.<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d398-f2"/></p>
                <p>Despatch will go forward in today’s pouch.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sheffield</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d398-f1" n="68" xml:id="d398-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it transmitted the translation of a memorandum left with the
                    Secretary of Industry, Commerce, and Labor by the Oil Producers’ Association on
                    June 24, 1926.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d398-f2" n="69" xml:id="d398-f2-content">
                <p>Neither printed; for instructions upon which they were based, see telegrams No.
                    212, June 21, and No. 241, July 20, to the Ambassador in Mexico, <ref
                        target="#pg679">pp. 679</ref> and <ref target="#pg680">680</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
